From e6aab88c095917cd4701fb9677e89a39f209b201 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tualua <dsp@mvdnet.org>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 13:45:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Wait for the VPP output syncpoint before encoding to avoid
 corrupted frames under concurrent sessions

When several QSVEncC sessions share one GPU, a single frame can be encoded
from a stale input surface: the last mini-GOP anchor before a forced IDR,
silently, with no error or warning. Observed with AV1 10-bit, --avhw with
VA video memory, a P010 VPP stage and B-pyramid, with 2 or more concurrent
sessions on an Arc A380 (Linux). See rigaya/QSVEnc#308.

Wait for the syncpoint attached to the incoming frame in
PipelineTaskMFXEncode::sendFrame() before calling EncodeFrameAsync(), and
return an error if the wait fails. The flush call passes no frame and is
unaffected. ffmpeg's QSV path, which does not show the problem, also waits
for each VPP output before encoding (there on the VPP side, in a retry loop).

With this change the corrupted frame appeared in 0 of 60 test iterations
(on 8.31), versus 13 of 40 hot-scene encodes without it. Because an
unrelated delay before EncodeFrameAsync() also hides the problem, this looks
like a timing window whose root cause probably lies below the API
(driver/runtime); this change is a workaround, not a proven fix of the
underlying cause.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 QSVPipeline/qsv_pipeline_ctrl.h | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/QSVPipeline/qsv_pipeline_ctrl.h b/QSVPipeline/qsv_pipeline_ctrl.h
index 8911f98a..42b03814 100644
--- a/QSVPipeline/qsv_pipeline_ctrl.h
+++ b/QSVPipeline/qsv_pipeline_ctrl.h
@@ -2863,6 +2863,16 @@ public:
             PrintMes(RGY_LOG_ERROR, _T("Invalid frame type.\n"));
             return RGY_ERR_UNSUPPORTED;
         }
+        // Wait for the producer's (VPP) syncpoint before handing the surface to the encoder.
+        // Workaround: with several sessions on one GPU, the encoder could otherwise encode a stale input surface (#308).
+        // Measurements show a timing window (an unrelated delay also hides it), so this is not a proven missing dependency.
+        if (frame) { // frame is nullptr on the flush call
+            auto err = frame->waitsync();
+            if (err != RGY_ERR_NONE) {
+                PrintMes(RGY_LOG_ERROR, _T("Failed to wait for the input frame before encoding: %s.\n"), get_err_mes(err));
+                return err;
+            }
+        }
 
         auto bsOut = m_bitStreamOut.get([enc = m_encode, log = m_log](RGYBitstream *bs) {
             mfxVideoParam par = { 0 };
